Original commit body from f6f755d5df4b9862496ce54b6ecb83bb6b586e43:

First producer migration onto the WI-zugob chokepoint. All four inline
minting sites in go.py -- net/http HandleFunc, the gorilla-mux builder
chain, chi/gorilla Handle, and the framework route path -- now call
analyze.base.make_route_symbol instead of hand-rolling the Symbol record.

CONSUMER-VISIBLE: a go route marker's Symbol.name changes from the handler
name ("submitHandler") to the method+path pair ("POST /submit"). That is
what java and javascript route markers have emitted since WI-tufil -- go
was the odd one out, and its id name-slot ALREADY carried "{METHOD} {path}"
while Symbol.name carried the handler, which is the ADR-0036 Ruling 1
violation this closes.

Handler identity is not lost: it stays in meta["handler_name"], the key
linkers/route_handler already resolved against (it never read Symbol.name),
so route->handler edge recovery is unchanged. 15 test assertions that read
routes[N].name are rewritten same-intent onto meta["handler_name"] -- they
still pin handler identification, they just read it where it now lives.

The change is forced rather than cosmetic: a multi-method registration
emits several markers at one span, so only the method+path pair keeps their
ids distinct.

RATCHET DRAINED. The strict xfail for `go` XPASSed on migration and failed
the suite until promoted out of NAME_SLOT_HOLES -- the gate working as
designed, and the reason it was landed before the sweep.

Behavioral evidence, real repo (~/ALL_REPOS/curriculum_repos/caddy):
3 route markers, 0 violations across all four properties -- id name-slot
round-trip, id kind-slot == Symbol.kind, non-empty origin_run_id joining a
real AnalysisRun, registered origin pass-id. Fixture corpus: go/java/
javascript all clean.

Remaining producers (py.py Django/Starlette/Flask-RESTful, ruby, php,
elixir, swift, play_routes, js_ts, grpc, annotation_convention) stay on
WI-zugob. py.py additionally needs new route-parity fixtures before it can
be gated -- the existing python fixture is Flask and produces no standalone
marker.

